  <section class="tb-section tb-ladder-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The cool diagram</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The descent into resistance.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="tb-sub">Every dropped dose, every unfinished course, teaches the bacterium another drug. Resistance is a staircase going down — and at each step, fewer medicines remain and the cure gets longer, costlier, and less likely. Hover a step to read it.</p>
    <div class="tb-ladder" id="tbLadder"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="tb-section tb-mumbai">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Super-tuberculosis · Mumbai, 2011</span>
      <h2 class="disp">When the staircase runs out.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tb-mumbai-grid">
      <article class="tb-mumbai-lead">
        <p>In late 2011, Dr. Zarir Udwadia and colleagues at Mumbai’s Hinduja Hospital described a handful of patients whose tuberculosis was resistant to <b>every drug they could test</b> — first-line, second-line, all of it. The press called it <em>totally drug-resistant TB</em>. The World Health Organization declined to make the label official, in part because no lab tests every drug — but the meaning was plain: in one of the world’s densest cities, TB had walked back off the bottom step of the staircase.</p>
        <p>It was not a freak. It was manufactured — by broken drug supply, unregulated private prescribing, months-long treatments abandoned halfway, and the crowding that lets one cough seed a hundred lungs. Resistance is less a mutation than a mirror.</p>
      </article>
      <aside class="tb-mumbai-facts">
        <div class="tb-fact"><span class="n">~1 in 8</span><span class="k">of the world’s TB is in India</span></div>
        <div class="tb-fact"><span class="n">~410K</span><span class="k">drug-resistant cases a year, worldwide</span></div>
        <div class="tb-fact"><span class="n">~2 of 5</span><span class="k">drug-resistant cases that ever start treatment</span></div>
        <div class="tb-fact tb-fact-hope"><span class="n">BPaLM</span><span class="k">new regimen: even XDR, cured in ~6 months</span></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tb-section tb-close">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The reckoning</span>
      <h2 class="disp">Curable since 1943. Still number one.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="tb-close-line">A person dies of tuberculosis roughly every twenty-five seconds — of a disease we learned to cure eighty years ago, with drugs that cost pennies. TB is not a mystery of biology anymore. It is a measure of who we decide is worth saving. Consumption created the modern world; whether the modern world finishes it is a choice, not a discovery.</p>
